      "writtenOn": "2019-06-25T15:46:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think this command isn\u0027t still solid because we haven\u0027t thought through how to handle the case where live product_id !\u003d hardcoded product_id.\n\nOn start-up, we should make EC query all TCPCs to get all live data and cache them. If product_id doesn\u0027t match the hard-coded value, the live data should precede. If EC fails to get a live data, it should populate the cache with a hardcoded value.\n\nThis masks hardcoded data from FAFT. However, FAFT should corrupt TCPC firmware so that we can validate hardcoded data (and that\u0027s exactly how we want to use hardcoded data). In other words, if EC can\u0027t get a product_id when TCPC firmware is corrupted, FAFT should fail.\n\nWith all these, I think we should name uint8_t renew as \u0027renew\u0027 because it does renew the cache. If renew \u003d\u003d 0, it\u0027ll get a cached value. It\u0027s a switch for controlling cache not controlling the source. Whether a cache is populated from a live data or a hardcoded data is not a caller\u0027s concern.",
      "parentUuid": "f756d8bc_fdbab6d6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 5161,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 5165,
        "endChar": 2
      },
      "revId": "3336354e62a7380603febbe1ba24bb55e718e8d1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e2be34d6_08858a51",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 5165,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-06-25T18:12:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It seems like a risky idea to have FAFT intentionally corrupt TCPC FW.  Not all of the TCPCs are built with a lot of write cycles, and trying to ensure the test is corrupting the FW such that the product ID is bad but the chip is still okay to update back to good FW is likely beyond the scope of what we can reasonably do reliably.  \n\nAs another consideration, not all of the TCPCs are running update-able firmware so we wouldn\u0027t be able to use the corrupt FW path to test the hard coded value versus the chip\u0027s live ID for them.\n\nIt seems there are two callers (depthcharge and FAFT) which have good reason to care about whether the value returned is coming from the chip or an expected define.",
      "parentUuid": "cfefb29e_796bb9b7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 5161,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 5165,
        "endChar": 2
      },
      "revId": "3336354e62a7380603febbe1ba24bb55e718e8d1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ebe58c51_c277684f",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 5165,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-06-25T22:18:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "When we test TCPC firmware update, we have to consume a write cycle anyway. We will just consume write cycles twice as fast: once for successful update and once for corruption. FYI, that\u0027s how we test firmware update for AP and EC. We don\u0027t trust firmware update unless corruption is tested.\n\n\u003e product ID is bad but the chip is still okay to update back to good FW\n\u003e is likely beyond the scope of what we can reasonably do reliably\n\nI believe we want any update failure to be recoverable, meaning we have an opportunity to try flashing again. If not, updating TCPC firmware itself is too risky to even try. So, we can entirely corrupt firmware to emulate failure in reading product_id. We don\u0027t have to craft binary and partially corrupt it.\n\nWrite cycle overflow is another corner case we ideally want to test. There will be some lines of code (and system states) which we would never exercise otherwise.",
      "parentUuid": "e2be34d6_08858a51",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 5161,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 5165,
        "endChar": 2
      },
      "revId": "3336354e62a7380603febbe1ba24bb55e718e8d1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "672f601f_85bc2f04",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
